  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the XOR gate do in a half adder?",
      "a": "XOR, exclusive or, outputs 1 when exactly one input is 1. That matches the sum digit: 0 plus 1 is 1, but 1 plus 1 gives 0 in that column.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an XOR gate?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the AND gate do in a half adder?",
      "a": "The AND gate outputs 1 only when both inputs are 1. That is exactly when you need to carry: 1 plus 1 is 10 in binary, a 0 here and a 1 carried.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is there an AND gate in an adder?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the sum bit?",
      "a": "It is the digit written in the current column of the answer. For inputs 0 and 0, 0 and 1, 1 and 0, and 1 and 1, it gives 0, 1, 1 and 0.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the sum output show?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the carry bit?",
      "a": "It is the 1 passed to the next column, just like carrying a ten in ordinary addition. A half adder carries only when both inputs are 1, because 1 plus 1 is 10 in binary.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does 1 plus 1 make 10 in binary?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between a half adder and a full adder?",
      "a": "A half adder adds two bits. A full adder also adds a carry coming in from the column before, so full adders can be chained to add numbers with many digits.",
      "asks": [
        "How do computers add big numbers?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In binary, 1 plus 1 equals 10, which means two.",
    "A full adder can be built from two half adders and an OR gate."
  ],
